{"id": "f396493f-58fd-49bc-ac7d-9873f5c81312", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T08:42:40.903063", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "881026ef-a83b-43c1-ba5e-32853cd0086b", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-a79a641d-72ca-47f3-b442-848beea976ff", "tool_call": {"id": "call-a79a641d-72ca-47f3-b442-848beea976ff", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Initial inspection of PDF/ETTm1 design task\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## Initial inspection — PDF model on ETTm1 (design-only)\\n\\n### 1. Workspace inventory\\n`/workspace` contains **exactly one file**:\\n```\\n/workspace/task.json            (389 bytes, only file in the workspace)\\n```\\nThere is no `track.md`, no `README`, no `*.py`, no configs, no scripts, no CSV/npz data, no `.git`, and no nested directories (verified with `ls -la /workspace`, `find /workspace -maxdepth 10`, and `ls -la /`). `task.json` mentions a `documents.track.md` key but no such file actually exists on disk.\\n\\nQuoted verbatim from `/workspace/task.json`:\\n```\\n{\\n  \\\"question\\\": \\\"How do different period settings affect the performance of the PDF model on the ETTm1 dataset?\\\",\\n  \\\"context\\\": \\\"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\\\",\\n  \\\"documents\\\": {\\n    \\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\nThe string under `documents.track.md` is the only \\\"track-style\\\" content present; it explicitly states the task is design-only and that a repository/compute environment is not required. There is no source repository citation in the file, so all model-configuration defaults below are *inferred from canonical literature*, not read from this workspace.\\n\\n### 2. System / dependency status\\n- Python 3.12.3 available, pip 24.0 present.\\n- Of the relevant ML/scientific stack, only **PyYAML 6.0.1** is installed. **No numpy, pandas, scipy, scikit-learn, statsmodels, matplotlib, torch, or JAX/Flax** (`python3 -m pip list | grep -i -E \\\"torch|numpy|pandas|scipy|sklearn|matplotlib|statsmodels\\\" → only `PyYAML 6.0.1`).\\n- `socket` test confirmed network is offline (pyproject/pip-fetch not possible); bubblewrap, no GPU.\\n- 24 CPUs, ~128 GB RAM, ~700 GB free on `/workspace`.\\n- Implication: the design must be valid even if no ML framework ends up installed; if a real run is later attempted out-of-band, a fresh Python env with torch + numpy + pandas would need to be supplied.\\n\\n### 3. What \\\"PDF model\\\" can mean here\\nThere is **no PDF-model source code in the workspace**, so I cannot quote model code, defaults, or wrapper code. In the time-series-forecasting literature paired with ETTm1, the acronym \\\"PDF\\\" plausibly maps to one of:\\n- **Patch + Decomposition + Forecast** (PatchTST-family vocabulary: patching the series, then trend/seasonal decomposition, then forecasting).\\n- A specific paper/repo named \\\"PDF\\\" — e.g., a *Periodic Decomposition Forecaster* or a *Patch Decomposition Forecaster* (less canonical than PatchTST but referenced in some benchmarks).\\n- A *Probability Density Function*-style distributional/quantile forecaster where \\\"period\\\" controls the temporal granularity (less consistent with ETTm1's fixed 15-minute sampling).\\n\\nBecause the task literally asks about \\\"**period** settings\\\" affecting PDF on ETTm1, the interpretation most coherent with both the acronym and ETTm1 is the **Patch + Decomposition + Forecast family**, where the \\\"period knobs\\\" are:\\n- (a) the **seasonal period `M`** of the decomposition (trend/seasonal split via moving-average kernel of size `M`, or explicit period `M` in a DLinear/NLinear-style block);\\n- (b) the **patch length `P` and stride `S`** (PatchTST patching parameterization — `P` plays a role analogous to a period because it sets the fundamental token period);\\n- (c) optionally the **input subsampling rate** if \\\"period\\\" is meant as temporal sub-sampling (less natural for ETTm1 at fixed 15-minute cadence, but retained as a sweep axis);\\n- (d) the **lookback window `L`** — because `L` bounds the largest period the patches can coherently cover on ETTm1.\\n\\nI will treat the question as sweeping (a) and (b), with (d) as an interaction. I am **flagging this interpretation as an assumption**, not a fact, because the workspace contains no code to disambiguate.\\n\\n### 4. There are no source-defaults to quote — proposing canonical PatchTST-family defaults as a working basis\\nBecause there is no source code, no model script, no config, and no README, I cannot quote any original default hyperparameter. The following table lists the working defaults I propose as a *single, source-attributed* baseline reference set (these are the thuml/PatchTST-paper and Time-Series-Library defaults, adapted to ETTm1):\\n\\n| Knob | Proposed default | Source basis (canonical, not in this repo) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Features | 7 (6 \\\"High UseFul Load\\\" + OT) | ETTm1 standard, PatchTST |\\n| Split | train : val : test = 12 mo : 4 mo : 4 mo (ETTm1 fixed split) | Zhou et al. 2021 / Informer |\\n| seq_len / lookback `L` | 336 | PatchTST paper |\\n| pred_len horizon `H` | {96, 192, 336, 720} (single-horizon scaler per run; report per-horizon) | PatchTST / LTSF benchmark |\\n| Patch length `P` | 16 | PatchTST paper |\\n| Stride `S` | 8 | PatchTST paper |\\n| Decomposition seasonal period `M` (kernel) | 25 (DLinear/MICN hourly-equivalent) — sweep target | DLinear paper; MICN paper |\\n| Encoder | Transformer, d_model=128, n_heads=16, 3 layers | PatchTST |\\n| Optimizer | Adam, lr=1e-3 (PatchTST)/1e-4 (DLinear) | PatchTST / DLinear |\\n| Batch size | 32 (or 128) | PatchTST |\\n| Epochs | 100 with early-stopping patience 10 | PatchTST |\\n| Seeds | {0, 1, 2} | PatchTST |\\n| Normalization | RevIN (per-instance) | Kim et al. 2021 / RevIN, PatchTST default |\\n| Metric | MSE (primary) and MAE | LTSF community standard |\\n\\nBecause the **researcher owns the source-supported setup**, the *final* defaults will be fixed in the result stage based on their confirmation; today they are proposal-level only.\\n\\n### 5. High-level experiment design (no execution — plan only)\\n\\n**Research question.** How do different period-related hyperparameters in the PDF model affect forecasting performance on ETTm1?\\n\\n**Hypothesis (treat as assumption, not as confirmed fact).** Because the identity of \\\"PDF\\\" is ambiguous, the hypothesis is stated in terms of the Patch+Decomposition+Forecast family:\\n- (H1) Seasonal-decomposition period `M` ≈ ETTm1's daily cycle (96 steps at 15-min resolution) yields the best MSE/MAE on the long horizon; ratios close to multiples/factors of the seasonal cycle dominate.\\n- (H2) Patch length `P` and stride `S` interact with `M`: best when `P` is a small divisor of `M`, so each patch exactly contains an integer number of seasonal cycles.\\n- (H3) Lookback `L` has the weakest effect once it spans ≥ 2× the dominant period.\\n\\n**(a) Sweep knobs (the answer's spine).**\\n1. Decomposition seasonal period `M` ∈ {24, 48, 96, 144, 288} (covering sub-daily and multi-day at 15-min cadence; 96 = 1 day, 288 = 3 days).\\n2. Patch length `P` × stride `S` grid: `P ∈ {8, 16, 24, 32}`, `S ∈ {4, 8, P/2, P}` (≈ 12 combinations).\\n3. Lookback `L` ∈ {96, 192, 336, 720} (interacts with `M`; included at second-priority level).\\n4. (Optional, lower priority because ETTm1 is fixed-cadence) input sub-sampling factor `d ∈ {1, 2, 4}` (only if the researcher confirms the codebase supports subsampling).\\n\\n**(b) Candidate values to test.** As listed in (a): 5 periods × ≈ 12 (P,S) pairs × 4 lookahead values ≈ 240 grid configurations; with 3 seeds each = 720 runs. *Feasibility check below — this will not actually be run inside the bubblewrap environment.*\\n\\n**(c) Fixed controls (held constant across all runs).**\\n- ETTm1 official train/val/test split (12mo/4mo/4mo).\\n- Feature set: all 7 channels, target = OT, RevIN normalization.\\n- Encoder, optimizer (Adam, lr per knob above), batch size, dropout.\\n- Horizon `H ∈ {96, 192, 336, 720}` — run separate models per horizon (canonical PatchTST convention).\\n- Seeds: {0, 1, 2}; report mean ± std.\\n- Primary metrics: MSE and MAE on the test set; identical evaluation windows and identical padding at sequence boundaries.\\n- Hidden output: report MSE per horizon; aggregate with mean MSE across horizons as a secondary metric.\\n- Identical early-stopping protocol and patience for all runs.\\n\\n**(d) Analysis plan.**\\n1. **Main-effect plot**: MSE (and MAE) vs. `M`, separate line per horizon, separate panel per `P`.\\n2. **Interaction heatmaps of (P, S)** at each `M`, holding `L` and `H` fixed.\\n3. **Per-horizon breakdown**: same plots faceted by `H ∈ {96, 192, 336, 720}` to surface any `M × H` interaction (long-horizon horizons are typically more period-sensitive).\\n4. **Normalization**: rank-normalize MSE within each horizon before averaging, to avoid the smallest horizon dominating the main effect.\\n5. **Statistical test (cheap and standard)**: paired Wilcoxon over runs of the same `(P,S,L,H)` configuration at different `M`, or a 4-way repeated-measures ANOVA with random seed as a blocking factor. Treat as exploratory; multi-comparison corrected via Benjamini–Hochberg if quantifying which `M` is \\\"best\\\".\\n6. **Sanity cross-check**: confirm MSE magnitudes lie in the canonical ETTm1 band for the chosen hyperparameter regime; otherwise flag as a bug.\\n\\n**(e) Feasibility inside the current bubblewrap environment.**\\n- **Out of scope here**: this bubblewrap has no torch/numpy/pandas and network is disabled, so a from-scratch run is **not feasible in-workspace**. The design is the deliverable, per `task.json` (\\\"design-only\\\").\\n- **Out-of-band feasibility (estimated, not measured)**: 240 configs × 3 seeds × 4 horizons = 2,880 single-horizon training runs. On a CPU-only box, even a single-horizon PatchTST run on ETTm1 typically takes tens of minutes → thousands of CPU-hours. The realistic recommendation is therefore to **pre-screen with H=96 only and a smaller grid** (≈ 5 M × 4 P × 4 L × 3 seeds = 240 runs), then expand once a top-tier region is identified. Alternatively, weight tying across (P,S) and seeds to reduce run count further.\\n- The design above uses only the 24 CPU cores and no Python ML stack; the writeup/analysis itself can be done entirely with PyYAML + stdlib if needed (loss logs in JSON/CSV).\\n\\n### 6. Consequential assumptions, unknowns, risks\\n\\n**Hard unknowns (need the researcher's confirmation before final design):**\\n1. **Identity of \\\"PDF\\\" model.** My interpretation (Patch + Decomposition + Forecast) is a reasoned guess, not a fact read from code. Possible alternatives:\\n   - PDF = a specific paper's *Periodic Decomposition Forecaster* (has a single `period` integer, sweep is trivial);\\n   - PDF = a *Probability Density Function* distributional forecaster (then \\\"period\\\" might mean discretization period of the predicted density, not the seasonal period).\\n2. **Where \\\"period\\\" lives.** `period` may refer to one of: decomposition kernel length `M`, patch length `P`, lookback `L`, or an FFT top-k period (TimesNet-style). I am preparing for the most common reading (decomposition kernel ± patch length). Please confirm if not.\\n3. **Defaults chosen by the source.** Because no source code is in the workspace, all defaults in §4 are proxy defaults from PatchTST / Time-Series-Library literature, not the original PDF repo's defaults. The researcher should confirm or supply the exact defaults.\\n4. **Whether a recorded `track.md` is expected at a different path** (e.g., under `/docs`, `/research`, or to be supplied mid-task). Currently nothing of the sort exists.\\n\\n**Computability risks:**\\n5. The bubblewrap has **no numpy / torch / pandas** and **no network**, so a live \\\"smoke test\\\" of the pipeline inside the bubblewrap is not feasible. The deliverable is the design document, not an executed experiment.\\n6. A full 4-way sweep is too large for a typical CPU-only budget. The design must commit to a *screening-then-refine* strategy; the screening proposal in §5(e) is offered as the default but is itself a choice to be confirmed.\\n\\n**Methodological risks:**\\n7. **Confounding between knobs.** `P`, `S`, `M`, `L` are not orthogonal (e.g., `P` and `S` change token count, which changes effective batch size/epoch count). The analysis plan will need to explicitly attribute effects, e.g., by reporting at comparable token counts or by including total-token-count as a covariate.\\n8. **Horizon asymmetry.** ETTm1 MSE is dominated by the long-horizon runs; without rank-normalization within horizon, the main-effect plot will simply mirror the `H=720` curve.\\n9. **Seed budget.** Three seeds is the LTSF community minimum but gives limited power for paired Wilcoxon if the effect is small.\\n\\n**Assumptions I am making (separate from the task requirements):**\\n- A-1. \\\"PDF\\\" = Patch + Decomposition + Forecast family (alternative accepted on confirmation).\\n- A-2. ETTm1 official 12/4/4-month split is the intended split; no rolling-origin CV requested.\\n- A-3. RevIN per-instance normalization is acceptable; alternates (StandardScaler, no norm) can be added as ablations later if desired.\\n- A-4. The deliverable is a written design (per `task.json`); no Python execution is expected inside this turn.\\n\\n**Task requirements I am treating as fixed (not assumptions):**\\n- TR-1. Question: effect of period settings on PDF on ETTm1.\\n- TR-2. Design-only — no execution claim.\\n- TR-3. Hold a single installation/runtime set (bubblewrap, no network, CPU) as a constraint, not a tunable.\\n\\n### Decision I need from the researcher before producing the full design\\nA