AE on the long horizon; ratios close to multiples/factors of the seasonal cycle dominate.\n- H2 Patch length P and stride S interact with M: best when P is a small divisor of M, so each patch exactly contains an integer number of seasonal cycles.\n- H3 Lookback L has the weakest effect once it spans ≥ 2× the dominant period.\n\n(a) Sweep knobs (the answer's spine).\n1. Decomposition seasonal period M ∈ {24, 48, 96, 144, 288} (covering sub-daily and multi-day at 15-min cadence; 96 = 1 day, 288 = 3 days).\n2. Patch length P × stride S grid: P ∈ {8, 16, 24, 32}, S ∈ {4, 8, P/2, P} (≈ 12 combinations).\n3. Lookback L ∈ {96, 192, 336, 720} (interacts with M; included at second-priority level).\n4. (Optional, lower priority because ETTm1 is fixed-cadence) input sub-sampling factor d ∈ {1, 2, 4}, only if the researcher confirms the codebase supports subsampling.\n\n(b) Candidate values to test. As listed in (a): ≈ 5 periods × ≈ 12 (P,S) pairs × 4 lookahead values ≈ 240 grid configurations; with 3 seeds each = 720 single-horizon training runs. Feasibility check below — will not be executed inside the bubblewrap environment.\n\n(c) Fixed controls (held constant across all runs).\n- ETTm1 official train/val/test split (12 mo / 4 mo / 4 mo).\n- Feature set: all 7 channels, target = OT, RevIN per-instance normalization.\n- Encoder, optimizer (Adam, lr per knob above), batch size, dropout — held constant.\n- Horizon H ∈ {96, 192, 336, 720} — run separate models per horizon (canonical PatchTST convention).\n- Seeds: {0, 1, 2}; report mean ± std.\n- Primary metrics: MSE and MAE on the test set; identical evaluation windows and identical padding at sequence boundaries.\n- Hidden output: report MSE per horizon; aggregate with rank-normalized mean MSE across horizons as a secondary metric.\n- Identical early-stopping protocol and patience for all runs.\n\n(d) Analysis plan.\n1. Main-effect plot: MSE (and MAE) vs. M, separate line per horizon, separate panel per P.\n2. Interaction hea