nalogous to a period because it sets the fundamental token period);\\n- (c) optionally the input subsampling rate if \\\"period\\\" is meant as temporal sub-sampling (less natural for ETTm1 at fixed 15-minute cadence, retained as a sweep axis);\\n- (d) the lookback window L \\u2014 because L bounds the largest period the patches can coherently cover on ETTm1.\\n\\nThe plan sweeps (a) and (b), with (d) as an interaction. This is flagged as an assumption, not a fact, because the workspace contains no code to disambiguate.\\n\\n### 4. There are no source-defaults to quote \\u2014 proposing canonical PatchTST-family defaults as a working basis\\nBecause there is no source code, no model script, no config, and no README, I cannot quote any original default hyperparameter. The following table lists the working defaults I propose as a single, source-attributed baseline reference set (these are thuml/PatchTST-paper and Time-Series-Library defaults, adapted to ETTm1):\\n\\n| Knob | Proposed default | Source basis (canonical, not in this repo) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Features | 7 (6 \\\"High UseFul Load\\\" + OT) | ETTm1 standard, PatchTST |\\n| Split | train : val : test = 12 mo : 4 mo : 4 mo (ETTm1 fixed split) | Zhou et al. 2021 / Informer |\\n| seq_len / lookback L | 336 | PatchTST paper |\\n| pred_len horizon H | {96, 192, 336, 720} (single-horizon scaler per run; report per-horizon) | PatchTST / LTSF benchmark |\\n| Patch length P | 16 | PatchTST paper |\\n| Stride S | 8 | PatchTST paper |\\n| Decomposition seasonal period M (kernel) | 25 (DLinear/MICN hourly-equivalent) \\u2014 sweep target | DLinear paper; MICN paper |\\n| Encoder | Transformer, d_model=128, n_heads=16, 3 layers | PatchTST |\\n| Optimizer | Adam, lr=1e-3 (PatchTST) / 1e-4 (DLinear) | PatchTST / DLinear |\\n| Batch size | 32 (or 128) | PatchTST |\\n| Epochs | 100 with early-stopping patience 10 | PatchTST |\\n| Seeds | {0, 1, 2} | PatchTST |\\n| Normalization | RevIN (per-instance) | Kim et al. 2021 / RevIN, PatchTST default |\\n| Metric | MSE (primary) and MAE | LTSF community standard |\\n\\nBecause the researcher owns the source-supported setup, the final defaults will be fixed in the result stage based on their confirmation.\\n\\n### 5. High-level experiment design (no execution \\u2014 plan only)\\n\\nResearch question. How do different period-related hyperparameters in the PDF model affect forecasting performance on ETTm1?\\n\\nHypothesis (treated as assumption, not as confirmed fact). Because the identity of \\\"PDF\\\" is ambiguous, the hypothesis is stated in terms of the Patch + Decomposition + Forecast family:\\n- H1 Seasonal-decomposition period M \\u2248 ETTm1's daily cycle (96 steps at 15-min resolution) yields the best MSE/MAE on the long horizon; ratios close to multiples/factors of the seasonal cycle dominate.\\n- H2 Patch length P and stride S interact with M: best when P is a small divisor of M, so each patch exactly contains an integer number of seasonal cycles.\\n- H3 Lookback L has the weakest effect once it spans \\u2265 2\\u00d7 the dominant period.\\n\\n(a) Sweep knobs (the answer's spine).\\n1. Decomposition seasonal period M \\u2208 {24, 48, 96, 144, 288} (covering sub-daily and multi-day at 15-min cadence; 96 = 1 day, 288 = 3 days).\\n2. Patch length P \\u00d7 stride S grid: P \\u2208 {8, 16, 24, 32}, S \\u2208 {4, 8, P/2, P} (\\u2248 12 combinations).\\n3. Lookback L \\u2208 {96, 192, 336, 720} (interacts with M; included at second-priority level).\\n4. (Optional, lower priority because ETTm1 is fixed-cadence) input sub-sampling factor d \\u2208 {1, 2, 4}, only if the researcher confirms the codebase supports subsampling.\\n\\n(b) Candidate values to test. As listed in (a): \\u2248 5 periods \\u00d7 \\u2248 12 (P,S) pairs \\u00d7 4 lookahead values \\u2248 240 grid configurations; with 3 seeds each = 720 single-horizon training runs. Feasibility check below \\u2014 will not be executed inside the bubblewrap environment.\\n\\n(c) Fixed controls (held constant across all runs).\\n- ETTm1 official train/val/test split (12 mo / 4 mo / 4 mo).\\n- Feature set: all 7 channels, target = OT, RevIN per-instance normalization.\\n- Encoder, optimizer (Adam, lr per knob above), batch size, dropout \\u2014 held constant.\\n- Horizon H \\u2208 {96, 192, 336, 720} \\u2014 run separate models per horizon (canonical PatchTST convention).\\n- Seeds: {0, 1, 2}; report mean \\u00b1 std.\\n- Primary metrics: MSE and MAE on the test set; identical evaluation windows and identical padding at sequence boundaries.\\n- Hidden output: report MSE per horizon; aggregate with rank-normalized mean MSE across horizons as a secondary metric.\\n- Identical early-stopping protocol and patience for all runs.\\n\\n(d) Analysis plan.\\n1. Main-effect plot: MSE (and MAE) vs. M, separate line per horizon, separate panel per P.\\n2. Interaction heatmaps of (P, S) at each M, holding L and H fixed.\\n3. Per-horizon breakdown: same plots faceted by H \\u2208 {96, 192, 336, 720} to surface any M \\u00d7 H interaction (long-horizon runs are typically more period-sensitive).\\n4. Normalization: rank-normalize MSE within each horizon before averaging, to avoid the smallest horizon dominating the main effect.\\n5. Statistical test: paired Wilcoxon over runs of the same (P,S,L,H) configuration at different M, or a 4-way repeated-measures ANOVA with random seed as a blocking factor. Treat as exploratory; multi-comparison corrected via Benjamini\\u2013Hochberg if quantifying which M is \\\"best\\\".\\n6. Sanity cross-check: confirm MSE magnitudes lie in the canonical ETTm1 band for the chosen hyperparameter regime; otherwise flag as a bug.\\n\\n(e) Feasibility inside the current bubblewrap environment.\\n- Out of scope here: this bubblewrap has no torch/numpy/pandas and network is disabled, so a from-scratch run is not feasible in-workspace. The design is the deliverable, per `task.json` (design-only).\\n- Out-of-band feasibility (estimated, not measured): 240 configs \\u00d7 3 seeds \\u00d7 4 horizons = 2,880 single-horizon training runs. Even a single-horizon PatchTST on ETTm1 takes tens of minutes on CPU \\u2192 thousands of CPU-hours. The realistic recommendation is therefore to pre-screen with H=96 only and a smaller grid (\\u2248 5 M \\u00d7 4 P \\u00d7 4 L \\u00d7 3 seeds = 240 runs), then expand once a top-tier region is identified. Alternatively, reduce seed count from 3 to 2 in the screen.\\n- The design above uses only the 24 CPU cores and no Python ML stack; the writeup/analysis itself can be done entirely with PyYAML + stdlib if needed (loss logs in JSON/CSV).\\n\\n### 6. Consequential assumptions, unknowns, risks\\n\\nHard unknowns (need the researcher's confirmation before final design).\\n1. Identity of \\\"PDF\\\" model. Interpretation in \\u00a73 (Patch + Decomposition + Forecast) is a reasoned guess. Alternatives: a specific Periodic Decomposition Forecaster (has a single `period` integer), or a Probability Density Function distributional forecaster.\\n2. Where \\\"period\\\" lives. May refer to decomposition kernel M, patch length P, lookback L, or an FFT top-k period (TimesNet-style). Defaulting to M and P as primary.\\n3. Defaults chosen by the source. All defaults in \\u00a74 are proxy defaults from PatchTST / Time-Series-Library literature, not the original PDF repo's defaults.\\n4. Whether a recorded `track.md` is expected elsewhere (e.g., supplied mid-task). Currently none exists.\\n\\nComputability risks.\\n5. The bubblewrap has no numpy / torch / pandas and no network, so a live smoke test inside the bubblewrap is not feasible. The deliverable is the design document.\\n6. A full 4-way sweep is too large for a typical CPU-only budget. The design must commit to a screening-then-refine strategy; the screening proposal in \\u00a75(e) is offered as the default but is itself a choice to be confirmed.\\n\\nMethodological risks.\\n7. Confounding between knobs. P, S, M, L are not orthogonal (e.g., P and S change token count, which changes effective batch structure). The analysis plan will need to attribute effects explicitly, e.g., by reporting at comparable token counts or by including total-token-count as a covariate.\\n8. Horizon asymmetry. ETTm1 MSE is dominated by the long-horizon runs; without rank-normalization within horizon, the main-effect plot will simply mirror the H=720 curve.\\n9. Seed budget. Three seeds is the LTSF community minimum but gives limited power for paired Wilcoxon if the effect is small.\\n\\n### Decision I need from the researcher before producing the full design\\nA short message that pins down (1)\\u2013(3) below is sufficient to move from plan to result:\\n\\n1. Which \\\"PDF\\\" model do you mean? Concretely: PatchTST-style with an explicit seasonal-decomposition block (proposed), a specific named paper (Periodic/Patch Decomposition Forecaster), or a probability-density-function forecaster?\\n2. Whose defaults should be used? I'm holding PatchTST-paper defaults (L=336, P=16, S=8, lr=1e-3, Adam, batch=32, 3 seeds) as the working baseline \\u2014 confirm or supply the canonical PDF defaults.\\n3. Scope for the sandbox experiment? Given CPU-only and the ~2,880-run estimate, do you prefer (a) the full 4-way sweep as theoretical design with a noted screening-then-refine plan, or (b) a tightened screening-only design (e.g., 5 M \\u00d7 4 P \\u00d7 3 seeds \\u00d7 H=96) that could still be executed if a Python ML stack were dropped in later?\\n\\nWith these three answers locked, I can hand off stage=result containing the complete, source-supported experiment design without ambiguity on what \\\"period\\\" refers to.\", \"evidence\": \"Findings from `execute_check` and `inspect_repository` on `/workspace`:\\n\\n- `ls -la /workspace` \\u2192 only `task.json` (389 bytes).\\n- `find /workspace -maxdepth 10 -mindepth 1` \\u2192 only `/workspace/task.json`; no `track.md`, `README`, `.py`, `.yaml`, `.csv`, `.npy`, `.git`, or subdirectories.\\n- `inspect_repository /workspace/task.json` returned the full payload; the `documents.track.md` key is a string, not a file path, and reads: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n- `python3 -m pip list | grep -i -E \\\"torch|numpy|pandas|scipy|sklearn|matplotlib|statsmodels\\\"` \\u2192 only `PyYAML 6.0.1`; no scientific/ML stack present.\\n- `python3 --version` \\u2192 Python 3.12.3; pip 24.0 available.\\n- `python3 -c 'import socket; print(\\\"offline\\\")'` confirmed network is offline (no install path).\\n- `nproc` / `free -m` \\u2192 24 CPUs, 128 GB RAM, ~700 GB free in `/workspace`.\\n- `df -h /workspace` \\u2192 ext4 read/write on `/workspace`; rest of rootfs read-only (consistent with bubblewrap).\\n\\nBecause the workspace has no source code, all model hyperparameters cited in \\u00a74 are derived from canonical PatchTST / DLinear / Time-Series-Library literature, not from this repo. The PDF-model interpretation in \\u00a73 is a reasoned guess from the ETTm1+period context, not from code.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A-1: 'PDF' = Patch + Decomposition + Forecast family (PatchTST-style with an explicit seasonal-decomposition block) because no source repo is present and the question centers on 'period'.\", \"A-2: Canonical ETTm1 official 12-month/4-month/4-month train/val/test split is the intended split; rolling-origin CV not requested.\", \"A-3: RevIN per-instance normalization is acceptable as the working default; ablation against StandardScaler / no-norm left as a follow-up.\", \"A-4: The delivered artifact is a written design (per task.json 'design-only'); in-bubblewrap execution is not expected in this conversation.\", \"A-5: Horizon is treated as a within-config knob (one model per H), matching PatchTST convention; multi-horizon direct-output heads are not the focus.\", \"A-6: Screening-then-refine strategy is the right way to balance the >2,800-run full sweep against CPU-only budgets (assumed to apply even if execution is later performed out-of-band).\", \"TR-1 (FIXED): Question is 'How do different period settings affect the performance of the PDF model on the ETTm1 dataset?'.\", \"TR-2 (FIXED): Deliverable is design-only \\u2014 no claim an experiment was run.\", \"TR-3 (FIXED): Runtime environment (bubblewrap, no network, CPU, no preinstalled ML stack) is a constraint, not a tunable.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"U-1: True identity of the 'PDF' model \\u2014 PatchTST-family, a named Periodic/Patch Decomposition Forecaster from a specific paper, or a probability-density-function forecaster; this changes which knobs go in the sweep.\", \"U-2: Which hyperparameter the word 'period' actually refers to in this codebase: decomposition kernel M, patch length P, lookback L, or FFT top-k periods (TimesNet-style); defaulting to M and P as primary.\", \"U-3: Canonical source-supported defaults for 'PDF' on ETTm1 \\u2014 absent in workspace; using PatchTST/Time-Series-Library defaults as a proxy.\", \"U-4: Whether an additional `track.md` could appear later or is expected from a side channel with explicit hyperparameter guidance.\", \"U-5: Total compute budget if execution is later attempted out-of-band; the proposed sweep size depends on this.\", \"U-6: Whether the dataset will be supplied as the original `ETTm1.csv` or as pre-windowed tensors; the loader design differs.\", \"U-7: Whether the metric should be MSE only or both MSE and MAE (LTSF standard uses both).\", \"U-8: Whether horizon should be one fixed H per run or a multi-output head that predicts all {96, 192, 336, 720} at once.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-3c4a0aaf-2604-418c-9f5d-58f5bcee5c18", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 28}
