{"id": "08b42ffa-1c34-4411-b57b-fd68dd7067b2", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T08:44:56.915291", "source": "user", "parent_id": "f403626e-a697-46df-9ddc-29b6c31c92ea", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Strong inspection — clean and honest that there's no source repo. I won't approve the approach until three ambiguities are pinned down, because they materially change which knobs go in the sweep and what 'period' even means. Please answer ONLY the three clarification questions below in your next turn; do not start writing the final design yet. After I get your answers, I will either approve the approach or ask for one more focused clarification. Note: per the submission contract the deliverable is design-only, so your answers shape the design document, not a live run. (1) Identity of 'PDF model'. Your §3 lists three plausible meanings. Confirm which one I should design around: (a) A PatchTST-style model with an explicit trend/seasonal decomposition block (your H1/H2 hypothesis path — M is the moving-average kernel, P and S are patch parameters); (b) A specific named PDF paper/repo you can name (e.g., PDForecaster / Periodic Decomposition Forecaster / Patch Decomposition Forecaster) — if so, name it so I can attribute the architecture; or (c) A probability-density / distributional forecaster (e.g., TDM, TPIN, density-head models) where 'period' would mean something different (FFT bins, quantile basis, or temporal granularity). My default in absence of an answer is (a). (2) Which hyperparameter does the word 'period' denote in this codebase? Concretely pick one primary axis and one secondary axis from: decomposition seasonal period M (kernel size), patch length P, patch stride S, lookback L, FFT top-k periods (TimesNet-style), or temporal subsampling factor. My default if unspecified is M as primary and P as secondary, with L as a measured covariate — but I would rather lock this from a source-supported cue than from my guess. (3) Source-supported defaults. You are right that nothing in /workspace gives us original PDF defaults. Two sub-questions: (3a) Are you OK with PatchTST-paper + Time-Series-Library defaults as the documented working baseline (L=336, P=16, S=8, Adam lr=1e-3, batch 32 per channel or 32, 100 epochs with early-stopping patience 10, RevIN, 3 seeds {0,1,2}, MSE+MAE, horizons {96,192,336,720} one-model-per-horizon, ETTm1 official 12/4/4 month split)? If not, supply the canonical PDF defaults you want me to use. (3b) For the screening-then-refine strategy: do you want (i) the full 4-way sweep documented theoretically with a noted screening phase, or (ii) a tightened screening-only design (e.g., 5 M × 4 P × 4 L × 3 seeds at H=96 only) that is plausibly executable on CPU if a Python ML stack were later supplied? My default in absence of an answer is (ii), because even a full PatchTST sweep on ETTm1 on CPU is thousands of CPU-hours and likely out of scope. Once you answer (1)–(3) I will approve the approach and request the full design document (rationale, fixed controls, sweep grid, analysis plan, statistical tests, feasibility). Do not pre-build the design before I approve.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 30}
{"id": "74b1416f-a4e8-4299-a120-2daae79ad44d", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T08:45:42.222156", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "08b42ffa-1c34-4411-b57b-fd68dd7067b2", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## Answers to the three clarification questions\n\n> Note (provenance): the `/workspace` contains only `task.json`. There is no source code, model script, README, or hyperparameter record I can quote to ground these answers. Each answer below is therefore a working decision with rationale and the residual uncertainty, not a fact read from a repository.\n\n### (1) Identity of \"PDF model\"\n\n**Working decision: (a) PatchTST-style model with an explicit trend/seasonal decomposition block**, matching your default.\n\nReasoning:\n- The acronym \"PDF\" pairs most parsimoniously with \"**P**atch + **D**ecomposition + **F**orecast\" in the LTSF literature; this is the dominant PatchTST-family vocabulary and is the family that (i) is benchmarked on ETTm1 by default and (ii) has a knob genuinely called \"period\" (the decomposition-block seasonal length).\n- Pros: aligns with your default, supports the H1/H2 hypothesis path I drafted, and produces a sensible sweep grid.\n- Cons I am acknowledging: I cannot verify from this workspace that \"PDF\" was defined this way; if you intended a specific paper (e.g., \"PDForecaster\" or \"Periodic Decomposition Forecaster\" or \"Patch Decomposition Forecaster\") and can name it, I will switch the attribution and any so-named paper-specific knobs in the final design.\n- I am rejecting (c) (probability-density / distributional forecaster like TDM/TPIN) because those interpret \"period\" as FFT-bin count or quantile granularity, which does not match the natural-language phrasing of your question (\"period settings\" on ETTm1).\n\nIf you want (b), please name the paper/repo and I will lock the architecture to it.\n\n### (2) Which hyperparameter does \"period\" denote?\n\n**Working decision: primary axis = decomposition seasonal period `M` (kernel size); secondary axis = patch length `P`. Lookback `L` is a measured covariate, not a swept axis of \"period\".**\n\nReasoning:\n- `M` is the only knob that the literature literally calls \"the period\" in trend/seasonal decomposition blocks (DLinear seasonal kernel, Autoformer series decomposition block, FEDformer, MICN multi-scale conv kernel). Sweeping `M` most directly answers the prompt.\n- `P` is the second-strongest analogue: in PatchTST each patch of length `P` becomes the fundamental token period, so the prompt plausibly also targets `P`.\n- I am pulling `L`, `S`, FFT-top-k, and subsampling out of the swept \"period\" axes because they belong to different underlying mechanisms (memory, tokenization, frequency discovery, sampling) and folding them in would confound the period answer. They will appear as fixed controls or measurement covariates.\n- Residual uncertainty: I cannot prove this currency from the source. If your codebase flags `P` or `S` as `period` in the config schema, please tell me and I will swap primacy.\n\n### (3) Source-supported defaults\n\n**(3a) Working baseline defaults.**\n\n**Working decision: yes, lock PatchTST-paper + Time-Series-Library defaults as the documented working baseline**, with two small explicit caveats I want captured in the design so the attribution stays honest:\n- Caveat-i: these defaults belong to PatchTST / TSlib, not to PDF; the design will state this explicitly. They are the cleanest defensible proxy in the absence of original PDF defaults.\n- Caveat-ii: I will use **batch 32** (PatchTST-paper convention, per-channel) — not \"32 per channel\" verbatim — and **Adam `lr = 1e-3`** for the Transformer-based PDF body. If the codebase prefers `lr = 1e-4` (DLinear-style for a simple-decomposition body), I will switch the optimizer block accordingly when you confirm the architecture.\n\nWorking baseline to be locked into the design:\n\n| Field | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Dataset | ETTm1 (15-min cadence), 7 channels, target OT |\n| Split | 12 mo train / 4 mo val / 4 mo test (official) |\n| Lookback `L` | 336 |\n| Horizon `H` | {96, 192, 336, 720}, one model per H |\n| Patch length `P` | 16 |\n| Stride `S` | 8 |\n| Seasonal period `M` | patched (see gate below) |\n| Normalization | RevIN (per-instance) |\n| Encoder (fixed block) | Transformer, d_model=128, n_heads=16, 3 layers |\n| Optimizer | Adam, lr=1e-3 (Transformer body) |\n| Batch size | 32 |\n| Epochs / patience | 100 / 10 early-stop |\n| Seeds | {0, 1, 2} |\n| Metrics | MSE (primary) and MAE |\n\n**M-gating rule for the design.** Because (3a) and (3b) conflict on `M` (PatchTST paper does not even have an explicit decomposition `M`), the design will treat `M` as the **sweep axis** and explicitly state that no single \"default `M`\" exists in the baseline-paper scope, listing the candidate values to be swept at the working-default point of `L=336, P=16, S=8`.\n\n**(3b) Sweep scope.**\n\n**Working decision: (ii) tightened screening-only design, plausibly executable on CPU if a Python ML stack were later supplied.**\n\nConcrete proposal for the screening tier (will not be executed here, only designed):\n\n- Phase 1 (screening, executable on CPU if a stack were supplied): `M ∈ {24, 48, 96, 144, 288}` × `P ∈ {8, 16, 24, 32}` × `L ∈ {96, 192, 336, 720}` × 3 seeds, all horizons `H ∈ {96, 192, 336, 720}` (single-horizon models). That is 5 × 4 × 4 × 3 = 240 single-horizon runs per horizon × 4 horizons = 960 single-horizon runs total. Still large, so I will recommend a *further* reduction: Phase 1a at `H=96` only (60 runs) to identify candidate M and P, then Phase 1b expanding the top-tier cells to all four H and 3 seeds. That gives ~60 + ~4 cells × 4 H × 3 seeds = ~108 runs as the realistic executable tier.\n- Phase 2 (refine, optional, on a real cluster): add FFT top-k baselines and stride sweep at the top-M cells.\n- Rationale: a full 4-way sweep with 4 horizons × 3 seeds is thousands of CPU-hours on ETTm1 even for a small PatchTST, which is out of scope per the submission contract. The screening tier preserves the cardinal power of the main effect (`M`) and the interaction (`P × M`) at a tractable budget.\n- Alignment with your default: yes, matches exactly.\n\n### What I will *not* do in this turn\n- I will not draft the design document. Per your instruction, the design is held until you approve.\n- I will not start a sweep, run any code, or pre-build sweep grids.\n\n### What I need from you to lock the plan and move to `stage=result`\nA single short confirmation of:\n1. (1) is locked to **(a) PatchTST-style with explicit trend/seasonal decomposition**, or you name a specific PDF paper/repo for (b).\n2. (2) is locked to **primary = `M`, secondary = `P`, `L` as covariate**, or you re-rank.\n3. (3a) is locked: PatchTST/TSlib defaults as documented working baseline (with the caveats above), or you supply canonical PDF defaults.\n4. (3b) is locked to: **screening-then-refine, executable-tier ~108 runs**, or you request the larger theoretical 4-way sweep.\n\nOnce those four items are locked I will produce the full design document in `stage=result`.", "evidence": "Same evidence base as the previous turn (no new commands run):\n- `ls -la /workspace` and `find /workspace -maxdepth 10` confirm only `task.json` is present; no source code, no `track.md`, no README, no `.yaml`, no `.py`, no CSV/NPZ data.\n- `inspect_repository /workspace/task.json` is the only authorable source document; its quoted `documents.track.md` string is \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" — no PDF-model identifier, no defaults, no split policy.\n- `python3 -m pip list | grep -i -E \"torch|numpy|pandas|scipy|sklearn|matplotlib|statsmodels\"` → only `PyYAML 6.0.1` available; bubblewrap with network disabled.\n- `nproc` / `free -m` → 24 CPUs, 128 GB RAM, ~700 GB free on `/workspace`.\n\nBecause none of the answers in this turn are sourced from a repository file, each one is presented above as a working decision with its rationale and residual uncertainty. No experiments were run; no sweep was executed; no design document drafted in this turn (per the researcher's explicit instruction).", "assumptions": ["A-1: 'PDF' = Patch + Decomposition + Forecast family (PatchTST-style with explicit seasonal-decomposition block); researcher default, accepted for design unless overridden.", "A-2: 'period' = decomposition seasonal period M as primary axis, patch length P as secondary axis, lookback L as a measured covariate rather than a swept 'period' axis.", "A-3: PatchTST-paper + Time-Series-Library defaults are an acceptable documented working baseline in the absence of original PDF defaults; caveated in the design.", "A-4: Horizon is one model per H from {96, 192, 336, 720} (single-horizon models), not a multi-output head.", "A-5: Screening-then-refine strategy is the right way to balance the >2,800-run theoretical full sweep against CPU-only budgets.", "A-6: The deliverable is a written design, not an executed run (per task.json and the submission contract).", "TR-1 (FIXED): Question is 'How do different period settings affect the performance of the PDF model on the ETTm1 dataset?'.", "TR-2 (FIXED): Deliverable is design-only — no claim an experiment was run.", "TR-3 (FIXED): Runtime environment (bubblewrap, no network, CPU, no preinstalled ML stack) is a constraint, not a tunable."], "uncertainties": ["U-1 (carried over, now addressed by choice): True identity of the 'PDF' model; locked to PatchTST-family unless researcher overrides with a named paper.", "U-2 (carried over, now addressed by choice): Which hyperparameter 'period' refers to; locked to M-primary / P-secondary / L-covariate unless reranked.", "U-3: Canonical source-supported defaults for 'PDF' on ETTm1 are absent in /workspace; PatchTST/TSlib defaults will be used as documented proxy in the design.", "U-4: Whether an additional `track.md` could appear later or be supplied mid-task from a side channel.", "U-5