{"id": "0d32d3a2-9a58-421d-8c8c-00c8fff13a92", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T08:46:03.004849", "source": "user", "parent_id": "e4668c3b-a70f-427b-9ba6-222f4401db30", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved with three working decisions locked and one residual risk I want captured in the design. Locked: (1) PDF = PatchTST-family with an explicit trend/seasonal decomposition block (your interpretation a); (2) primary 'period' axis = decomposition seasonal period M, secondary = patch length P, lookback L held as a measured covariate (re-ranked if I had wanted otherwise I would have said so); (3a) PatchTST-paper + Time-Series-Library defaults as the documented working baseline with the two caveats you raised (defaults belong to PatchTST/TSlib not PDF, and Adam lr=1e-3 applies to the Transformer body); (3b) screening-then-refine with the executable tier of Phase 1a (H=96 only, 5 M x 4 P x 3 seeds, in your accounting ~60 runs) followed by Phase 1b expansion at top-tier cells across all four H with 3 seeds (~108 runs total). One residual to keep in the design's 'assumptions and risks' section: because /workspace has no PDF source code, sections that quote defaults or architecture MUST be attributed to PatchTST / DLinear / TSlib with an inline marker that they are a proxy, not original PDF values; do not present them as if quoted from a PDF repo. Now produce the full design document covering, in this order: (a) research question and pre-registered hypotheses H1/H2/H3 stated as assumptions; (b) model assumed (PatchTST-style with explicit moving-average seasonal-decomposition block, RevIN per-instance norm, Transformer encoder d_model=128 n_heads=16 3 layers), with a one-paragraph note on what would change if 'PDF' later turns out to be a different named paper; (c) data: ETTm1 official 12/4/4-month split, 7 channels, target OT, loader behavior (windowed tensor vs raw CSV), padding at boundaries; (d) fixed controls table (L=336 baseline, S=8, optimizer Adam lr=1e-3, batch 32, epochs 100 patience 10, RevIN, seeds {0,1,2}, MSE+MAE, horizons {96,192,336,720} one model per H); (e) sweep grid: Phase 1a H=96 with M in {24, 48, 96, 144, 288} and P in {8, 16, 24, 32} and 3 seeds, Phase 1b top-tier cells expanded to all four H x 3 seeds, Phase 2 optional FFT top-k and stride refinement; (f) analysis plan: main-effect plot (MSE/MAE vs M, faceted by H, colored by P), interaction heatmap of (M,P) for each H, rank-normalization within horizon before averaging, paired Wilcoxon across M at fixed (P,L,H) with BH correction, sanity-band cross-check against canonical ETTm1 PatchTST MSE magnitudes; (g) feasibility on the 24-CPU bubblewrap given that no ML stack is installed (acknowledge the design itself is the deliverable per task.json and that a live run would need a later env with torch+numpy+pandas); (h) explicit assumptions and risks (U-1..U-8 carried from your U-list). Do not fabricate results. Do not claim any experiment was run. End with a short 'open questions for the researcher' paragraph listing the few items that would still need confirmation if execution were later attempted (e.g., exact data file format, whether lr should drop to 1e-4 for a pure-DLinear body variant, batch convention).\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 33}
{"id": "105c623c-f8d9-4387-a50d-3abf45b691a3", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T08:47:32.127573", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "0d32d3a2-9a58-421d-8c8c-00c8fff13a92", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experiment Design — Effect of Period Settings on PDF Model Performance on ETTm1\n\n> **Provenance and scope of this document.** `/workspace` contains only `task.json`; no model source code, no default-hyperparameter record, no dataset file, no README/track.md on disk. This design is therefore **design-only** and quotes no values as if read from a PDF repository. Every architectural and hyperparameter default is attributed to its canonical literature source with an inline proxy marker. Any run of this design is out of scope for this task per `task.json` (which states: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\").\n\n---\n\n## (a) Research question and pre-registered hypotheses\n\n**Research question.** How do different period-related hyperparameter settings affect the forecasting performance of the PDF (PatchTST-family) model on the ETTm1 dataset?\n\n**Hypotheses, stated as assumptions about the experiment (not as forecast of outcome):**\n\n- **H1 — Seasonal-decomposition period `M` matters most on the long horizon.** Best MSE/MAE are obtained when `M` is at or near a divisor/multiple of ETTm1's daily cycle (96 steps at 15-min resolution), and the magnitude of the `M` effect grows with horizon `H`.\n- **H2 — Patch length `P` interacts with `M`.** Best MSE/MAE occur when `P` is a small divisor of `M`, so each patch covers an integer number of seasonal cycles; non-divisory `P` increases aliasing of the decomposition.\n- **H3 — Lookback `L` is a covariate, not a driver.** Once `L ≥ 2 × max(M)`, varying `L` produces a smaller effect on rank-normalized MSE than varying `M` or `P`.\n\nThese hypotheses are **assumptions recorded up front** and will be evaluated by the analysis plan in §(f). They are not asserted as true.\n\n---\n\n## (b) Model assumed\n\n**Working model.** A PatchTST-style architecture with an **explicit trend/seasonal decomposition block** in the forecasting head — i.e., the input time series is patched with length `P` and stride `S`, the patches pass through a Transformer encoder, and the decoder output is split by a moving-average-based series decomposition into seasonal and trend components before being projected to the forecast horizon `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` `[TSlib proxy]`. Per-instance RevIN normalization is applied at the input and reversed at the output `[RevIN-paper proxy]`.\n\nConcretely, the components in scope are:\n\n| Component | Choice | Source attribution |\n|---|---|---|\n| Windowing / patching | patches of length `P`, stride `S` along the time axis | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n| Series decomposition | moving-average seasonal-trend split with kernel size `M` applied to the decoder output | `[DLinear-paper proxy]` for the block shape; `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` for the patched encoder feeding into it; `[TSlib proxy]` for the wiring |\n| Normalization | RevIN per-instance on the encoder input, reversed before loss | `[RevIN-paper proxy]` `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n| Encoder | Transformer, `d_model=128`, `n_heads=16`, `n_layers=3` | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n| Loss | MSE on normalized targets (RevIN-reduced scale) | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` `[TSlib proxy]` |\n| Output | single-horizon head per `H ∈ {96, 192, 336, 720}`, no multi-output heads | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n\n**Note on re-identification.** If \"PDF\" turns out to be a different named paper (e.g., a separately published *Periodic Decomposition Forecaster* or *Patch Decomposition Forecaster*), only the following items in this design would need to be revisited, and only minimally: (i) the decomposition block signature (whether it is a moving-average split, a learnable filter, or an FFT-bank reconstruction); (ii) the encoder dimension if the named paper uses a smaller body; (iii) the canonical default for the period knob (single integer vs. integer-valued set); and (iv) the data-loader signature. The sweep grid (M, P, L, seeds, horizons), the fixed controls table, the analysis plan, and the feasibility budget **would be substantively re-used**, because the research question — \"how does the period setting affect performance\" — is invariant under the architectural variant.\n\n---\n\n## (c) Data\n\n| Field | Value | Source attribution |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dataset | ETTm1 (15-minute cadence, multivariate) | `[Informer/paper proxy]` for ETTm1 |\n| Channels | 7 (6 \"High UseFul Load\" + 1 `OT`) | `[ETTm1 standard]` `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n| Target | `OT` channel for primary MSE/MAE | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n| Train / Val / Test split | 12 months / 4 months / 4 months (official) | `[Informer/paper proxy]` `[TSlib proxy]` |\n| Loader behaviour | if raw `ETTm1.csv`: load CSV, parse the date column, sort chronologically, treat as a `pandas`-style DataFrame, then slide windows of length `L+H` with stride 1 along time, slicing the last `H` timesteps as the ground-truth forecast and the leading `L` as the input; if pre-windowed tensors `*.npy`/`*.pt`: load with a channel axis preserved (shape `[N, 7, L+H]` or `[N, L+H, 7]`), no further windowing | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` `[TSlib proxy]` |\n| Boundary padding | **no zero-padding inside the test split**; for sliding the final window across the right edge of the test split, use RevIN with zero-padded left-context (i.e., warp with the last `L` training-side observations mirrored once, never with zeros) to avoid contamination-induced horizon bias | `[RevIN-paper proxy]` `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n| Normalization in loader | RevIN fits the mean/std on each input window only (per-instance) | `[RevIN-paper proxy]` |\n| Data leakage controls | (i) per-instance RevIN is fit **only** on the input window — never on the horizon; (ii) the moving-average decomposition `M` is applied at inference time only to the decoder output, never refit on the test interval; (iii) the training split is contiguous (12 mo), val split contiguous (4 mo, immediately following), test split contiguous (4 mo, immediately following) | `[Informer/paper proxy]` for split policy; `[RevIN-paper proxy]` for per-instance invariance |\n\nA concrete **loader pseudocode** (for a future implementer; **not** to be executed in this bubblewrap):\n\n```python\n# Pseudocode, attributed to PatchTST/TSlib/RevIN papers as proxy\ndef make_windowed_ettm1(csv_or_tensor_path, L, H, stride_train=1, stride_eval=H):\n    series = load_ettm1(csv_or_tensor_path)            # shape [T, 7] or [7, T]\n    train, val, test = official_split(series)          # 12mo / 4mo / 4mo\n    def to_windows(split, stride):\n        T = len(split); out = []\n        for s in range(0, T - L - H + 1, stride):\n            x = split[s : s + L]                       # [L, 7]\n            y = split[s + L : s + L + H, ot_idx]       # [H]\n            out.append((x, y))\n        if T - L - H + 1 <= 0:\n            # right-edge test window: mirror prior context, no zero-pad\n            x = np.concatenate([split[max(0, T - 2*L):T - L], split[T - L:]], axis=0)[-L:]\n            out.append((x, split[max(0, T - H):T, ot_idx]))\n        return out\n    return to_windows(train, stride_train), to_windows(val, stride_eval), to_windows(test, stride_eval)\n```\n\n---\n\n## (d) Fixed controls table\n\nThe following table is **held constant for every run**, including Phase 1a, Phase 1b, and Phase 2.\n\n| Field | Value | Source attribution / proxy marker |\n|---|---|---|\n| Dataset | ETTm1 | `[Informer/paper proxy]` |\n| Channels | 7; target `OT` | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n| Split | 12mo train / 4mo val / 4mo test | `[Informer/paper proxy]` |\n| Lookback `L` (baseline) | 336 — covariate, fixed at this value for Phase 1a/1b unless explicitly varied as covariate | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n| Stride `S` | 8 | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n| Horizons `H` | {96, 192, 336, 720}, one model per `H` | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` `[TSlib proxy]` |\n| Normalization | RevIN per-instance | `[RevIN-paper proxy]` |\n| Encoder | Transformer, `d_model=128`, `n_heads=16`, `n_layers=3` | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n| Optimizer | Adam, `lr=1e-3` for the Transformer body | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n| Batch size | 32 (joint across channels; per-batch count, not per-channel) | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` `[TSlib proxy]` |\n| Epochs | 100 | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n| Early stopping | patience 10 on val MSE | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n| Seeds | {0, 1, 2} | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\n| Primary metric | MSE on test set | LTSF community standard |\n| Secondary metric | MAE on test set | LTSF community standard |\n| Padding policy | no zero-padding inside the test split; mirror prior context at the right edge | `[RevIN-paper proxy]` |\n| Hardware target (out-of-band) | 24 CPU cores, no GPU; float32 | `[environment constraint]` |\n\n> **Proxy attestation.** Every row above that is **not** `environment constraint` or `LTSF community standard` is sourced from PatchTST, DLinear, RevIN, Informer, or Time-Series-Library papers as a documented working baseline. None of these is being asserted as the original PDF repository's choice.\n\n---\n\n## (e) Sweep grid\n\nThree phases. Phase 1a is the executable tier inside the screening plan. Phase 1b is the refinement tier. Phase 2 is optional and listed for future use.\n\n### Phase 1a — Screening at `H = 96`\n\n| Knob | Values |\n|---|---|\n| Decomposition seasonal period `M` | {24, 48, 96, 144, 288} (covering sub-daily to multi-day at 15-min cadence; 96 ≈ 1 day) |\n| Patch length `P` | {8, 16, 24, 32} |\n| Lookback `L` (covariate, held fixed at this phase) | 336 |\n| Stride `S` | 8 (fixed) |\n| Horizon `H` | 96 (fixed) |\n| Seeds | {0, 1, 2} |\n\n**Phase 1a run count.** 5 × 4 × 3 = **60 single-horizon training runs.**\n\n### Phase 1b — Top-tier expansion to all four horizons\n\nTop-tier definition (pre-registered before unblinding):\n\n- Compute rank-averaged MSE across the 60 Phase 1a runs.\n- Take the **top-2 values of `M`** by mean rank-MSE and the **top-2 values of `P`** by mean rank-MSE.\n- Take the **top-2 `(M, P)` interactions** by mean rank-MSE.\n- Combine via a deduplicated cell set: typically 2 M × 2 P → 4 cells, unless the interaction wins force more, capped at 6 cells.\n\n| Knob | Values |\n|---|---|\n| Cells | up to 6 `(M, P)` top-tier cells from Phase 1a |\n| Lookback `L` | 336 (fixed covariate baseline; **Optionally expand L with one secondary value**, see below) |\n| Horizon `H` | {96, 192, 336, 720} |\n| Seeds | {0, 1, 2} |\n\n**Phase 1b run count.**\n\n- If 4 cells: 4 × 4 × 3 = 48 runs.\n- If 5 cells: 5 × 4 × 3 = 60 runs.\n- If 6 cells: 6 × 4 × 3 = 72 runs.\n\nThe actual run count for Phase 1b is between **48 and 72**, depending on top-tier tie-breakers. **Planning budget = 48 runs (lower bound = 4 cells), giving an executable tier of 60 + 48 = 108 runs**, which is the figure approved in the plan.\n\n> **L-auxiliary measurement (optional, inside Phase 1b).** At the single top-tier `(M, P)` cell, additionally train at `L ∈ {96, 192, 336, 720}` to test H3 directly. Adds at most 1 × 4 L × 3 seeds = **12 auxiliary runs**. Reported as a covariate, not a primary sweep axis.\n\n### Phase 2 — Optional FFT-top-k and stride refinement (NOT in the executable tier; for future use)\n\n| Knob | Values (only at Phase-1b top-tier cells) |\n|---|---|\n| FFT top-k | {1, 3, 5} (TimesNet-style, for benchmarking only) |\n| Stride `S` | {4, 8, 16} (folded in here, not in Phase 1a/1b) |\n| Horizons `H` | all four |\n| Seeds | {0, 1, 2} |\n\n**Phase 2 run count.** Up to 6 cells × 3 top-k × 3 stride × 4 H × 3 seeds = **648 runs**, listed here only to bound the size of any future expansion; this is **not within the approved executable tier of 108 runs** and would require compute outside this task.\n\n### Total approved budget\n\n- **Approved executable tier:** 60 (Phase 1a) + 48 (Phase 1b lower bound) = **108 single-horizon runs**.\n- This is the figure the design should be evaluated against per the submission contract (design-only).\n\n---\n\n## (f) Analysis plan\n\nThe analysis is gated on Phase 1a / Phase 1b / (optional) Phase 2 results being available out-of-band; this design only specifies *what would be computed*, not that it was computed.\n\n1. **Main-effect plot (MSE and MAE vs. `M`).**\n   - x-axis: `M` ∈ {24, 48, 96, 144, 288}.\n   - y-axis: test-set MSE (or MAE).\n   - Line color: `P` (4 levels).\n   - Facets: one panel per `(H, L)` combination.\n   - y-error bars: standard deviation across the 3 seeds.\n\n2. **Interaction heat-maps of `(M, P)`.**\n   - For each `(H, L)` combination, draw a 5 × 4 heat-map with `M` on the y-axis, `P` on the x-axis, cell value = mean MSE across seeds.\n   - Secondary heat-map with rank-normalized MSE (see step 4).\n\n3. **Per-horizon breakdown.**\n   - The same heat-maps and main-effect plots faceted by `H ∈ {96, 192, 336, 720}` to surface any `M × H` interaction. Long-horizon runs are weighted equally only after rank normalization.\n\n4. **Rank normalization within horizon.**\n   - For each horizon `H`, rank all 60 (Phase 1a) cells by mean MSE across seeds; convert ranks to `[0, 1]` (or to percentiles). Use rank-normalized values for any cross-horizon aggregation or \"main effect averaging\" so the long-horizon runs do not dominate.\n   - When reporting any cross-horizon summary statistic, use rank-normalized values.\n\n5. **Statistical tests.**\n   - **Primary test: paired Wilcoxon signed-rank.** For each fixed `(P, L, H)` configuration, pair the seeds across the levels of `M` and apply a Wilcoxon test on seed-mean MSE. Report an effect-size (rank-biserial correlation) alongside the p-value.\n   - **Multi-comparison:** Benjamini–Hochberg correction across the in-hypothesis test family (per `(P, H)` strip), reporting `q`-values. Pre-register the alpha = 0.05 threshold for the BH rejection.\n   - **Secondary test:** a 4-way repeated-measures ANOVA over `(M, P, H, seed)` to detect interaction effects; **report as exploratory** because the residual degrees of freedom with 3 seeds are limited.\n   - **Pre-registered nulls:**\n     - H1: alternative = \"MSE differs in `M`\", aggregate test.\n     - H2: alternative = \"non-additive interaction of `M` × `P`\", interaction term in the ANOVA.\n     - H3: alternative = \"MSE changes with `L` at top `(M, P)`\", Wilcoxon on the L-auxiliary batch (Phase 1b optional extension).\n\n6. **Sanity-band cross-check.**\n   - Confirm that the **MSE magnitudes lie within the canonical PatchTST-on-ETTm1 band** for the chosen architecture at the working-baseline `(P=16, M=24, L=336, H=96, lr=1e-3, RevIN)` configuration. The expected band, sourced from the PatchTST and Time-Series-Library reports: `MSE ≈ 0.29–0.33` and `MAE ≈ 0.34–0.39` on ETTm1 at `(L=336, H=96)` `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` `[TSlib proxy]`. If the in-experiment value falls outside this band by more than ~25%, flag the configuration as suspect (likely a code-bug rather than a real PDF effect) before reporting the main effect.\n   - The sanity threshold is a proxy and double-coded; it is *not* an experimental claim.\n\n7. **Reporting artifacts (planned, not produced here).**\n   - One CSV with columns `{H, L, M, P, seed, MSE, MAE, rank_MSE_H}` for the entire run matrix.\n   - PNG main-effect plot and `(M, P)` heat-map for each `(H, L)` panel.\n   - JSON summary file with the BH-corrected Wilcoxon q-values per hypothesis per horizon.\n\n---\n\n## (g) Feasibility in the 24-CPU bubblewrap\n\n- The bubblewrap has **no scientific/ML stack installed** (only `PyYAML 6.0.1` is present in the relevant subset of `pip list`; no numpy, pandas, scipy, scikit-learn, statsmodels, matplotlib, torch, or JAX). Network is disabled, so no `pip install` can be performed inside the bubble.\n- Therefore, this design document is itself the deliverable for this turn (per `task.json` \"design-only\" and the submission contract). **No live run is claimed and none can be performed inside this environment.**\n- **Out-of-band feasibility** for the executable tier of 108 runs:\n  - On a CPU-only box (24 threads, no GPU), a single-horizon PatchTST on ETTm1 with `L=336`, `H ≤ 720` is on the order of tens of minutes per epoch at the smaller horizon and grows to hours at `H=720`. With the early-stopping budget of 100 epochs / patience 10, realistic per-run cost is 1–6 CPU-hours at `H=96` and 3–12 CPU-hours at `H=720`.\n  - The Phase 1a + Phase 1b 108-run tier is therefore estimated at ~250–700 CPU-hours of training (assuming most runs early-stop near patience 10). This is feasible in a few days on a 24-CPU box with PyTorch.\n  - The Phase-1b L-auxiliary 12-run extension is an extra ~50–150 CPU-hours.\n  - The optional Phase 2 (~648 runs) is **not recommended** on CPU alone; it would need a GPU cluster.\n- The design document does not depend on a particular ML stack — only the loader pseudocode, fixed-controls table, sweep grid, and analysis plan. These can be drafted without any framework installed.\n\n---\n\n## (h) Assumptions and risks\n\nCarried forward from the U-list and labelled inline:\n\n- **U-1 (resolved, kept as confirmation): 'PDF' = PatchTST-family with explicit decomposition block, locked by researcher.** Assumption; if a named paper is supplied later, only the architecture subscript in §(b) and the decomposition-block signature need revision.\n- **U-2 (resolved, kept as confirmation): 'period' axis = `M` (primary), `P` (secondary), `L` (covariate), locked by researcher.** Assumption; if the codebase later exposes `period` as a different axis, the sweep must re-rank.\n- **U-3: No canonical PDF defaults exist on disk.** All baselines quoted in this design are PatchTST, DLinear, RevIN, Informer, or Time-Series-Library paper proxies, marked inline. **Risk:** misattributing proxy defaults to PDF — mitigated by the inline `[proxy]` markers, which are required at every claim.\n- **U-4: No `track.md` exists on disk** (only a string inside `task.json`). **Risk:** the researcher could supply additional cue material mid-task; the design should be re-checked against any such addition.\n- **U-5: Out-of-band CPU budget may be smaller than the 250–700 CPU-hour Phase-1 estimate.** **Risk:** run starvation → incomplete main-effect plots. **Mitigation:** pre-register a strict priority order: Phase 1a first; Phase 1b second (only at cells whose Phase 1a MSE lies inside the top-quartile of rank-MSE); Phase 1b L-auxiliary third; Phase 2 not at all on CPU.\n- **U-6: ETTm1 data file format unknown (`ETTm1.csv` vs. pre-windowed tensors).** **Risk:** loader bug at the boundary-pad step. **Mitigation:** the loader pseudocode in §(c) handles both; a 1-row smoke test (load, slice a single window, verify shape `[L, 7]` and `[H]` for OT) is the recommended first integration step.\n- **U-7: Metric scope unclear.** **Risk:** if the source expects MSE only, the MAE column is dead weight; if it expects both, dropping MAE is a defect. **Mitigation:** keep both MSE (primary) and MAE (secondary) by default; this is the LTSF community standard.\n- **U-8: Batch convention unclear.** **Risk:** \"batch 32\" can mean 32 windows (joint across channels, default here) or 32 windows per channel. **Mitigation:** the design locks the joint convention; the open questions section asks the researcher to confirm.\n\n**Cross-cutting risks** (additional to U-1..U-8):\n\n- **Confounding between `P` and token count.** Different `(P, S)` choices change the number of tokens, which changes the encoder's effective depth and the optimizer's dynamics. **Mitigation:** report token counts in the CSV and as a covariate in §(f) step 5 if the ANOVA finds a `M × P` interaction.\n- **Horizon asymmetry dominates MSE magnitude.** Without rank normalization the main-effect plot mirrors `H=720`. **Mitigation:** step 4 in §(f) is non-optional.\n- **Three seeds give limited paired-test power.** Wilcoxon at 3-seed pairing is exact only for some configurations; for small effects, two-seed pilot followed by a power calculation may be needed before a third seed. Pre-registered option: collapse to median over seeds and report IQR if a third seed run is not feasible.\n- **No live integration test inside this bubblewrap.** **Risk:** silent loader/decoder bugs. **Mitigation (out-of-band):** formal smoke test of the loader + one 1-epoch training step at `(L=336, P=16, M=24, H=96, lr=1e-3)` before launching the Phase 1a grid.\n\n---\n\n## Open questions for the researcher (if a run is later attempted)\n\nThese items would still need one-line confirmation before the executable tier is launched in a future out-of-band environment; they are explicitly **not blocking** the design.\n\n1. **ETTm1 file format.** Original `ETTm1.csv` with date column, or pre-windowed tensors (`*.npy` / `*.pt`)? — needed for the loader smoke test.\n2. **LR for a DLinear-style body.** If the codebase variant of PDF swaps the Transformer encoder for a DLinear-style linear body, the optimizer LR should drop from `1e-3` to `1e-4` `[DLinear-paper proxy]`. Confirm which body the PDF implementation actually uses.\n3. **Batch convention.** \"Batch 32\" in §(d) is locked as 32 windows jointly across channels. Confirm if the codebase instead uses \"32 per channel\".\n4. **Phase 1b tie-breaker.** When two `M` values produce equal rank-MSE inside Phase 1a, the design keeps the smaller of the two. Confirm or supply an alternate tie-breaker.\n5. **Whether an additional `track.md`** with original PDF defaults is expected from the researcher mid-task; none exists on disk now.\n6. **Whether MSE only, MAE only, or both.** The design locks MSE (primary) + MAE (secondary); confirm.\n7. **Tolerance for the canonical-PatchTST sanity band** in §(f) step 6 (default ±25%). Tighten or loosen if a stricter experimental prior is required.\n\n---\n\n> **Closing attestation.** This document is a design, not a run. Every value attributed to a paper is a documented working baseline marked as such. No experiment was performed, no result was fabricated.", "evidence": "Evidence base carried in from earlier turns (no new commands needed to finalize a design-only document; no source files appeared, no dependencies installed):\n\n- `ls -la /workspace` and `find /workspace -maxdepth 10` show only `/workspace/task.json` — no source, no data, no README, no `track.md`.\n- `inspect_repository /workspace/task.json` quoted the only authorable source document (`question`, `context`, `documents.track.md` = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\").\n- `python3 -m pip list | grep -i -E \"torch|numpy|pandas|scipy|sklearn|matplotlib|statsmodels\"` printed only `PyYAML 6.0.1`, confirming no ML stack is installed.\n- `python3 -c 'import socket; print(\"offline\")'` and the bubblewrap spec confirm network is disabled → no `pip install` available.\n- `nproc` = 24, `free -m` reports ~128 GB RAM, `df -h /workspace` reports ~700 GB free — sufficient headroom for an out-of-band Phase 1a/1b run if a stack were supplied later.\n\nEvery numeric default in the design is therefore a *PatchTST-paper proxy* / *DLinear-paper proxy* / *RevIN-paper proxy* / *Informer/paper proxy* / *TSlib proxy* / *LTSF community standard* / *environment constraint*, and none is asserted as the original PDF repository's value.", "assumptions": ["Resolved A-1: 'PDF' = PatchTST-family with explicit trend/seasonal decomposition block (locked by researcher approval).", "Resolved A-2: 'period' axis = decomposition seasonal period M (primary), patch length P (secondary), lookback L (covariate) (locked by researcher approval).", "Resolved A-3: PatchTST-paper + Time-Series-Library defaults are the documented working baseline, with explicit caveats that they belong to PatchTST/TSlib and not to PDF (locked by researcher approval).", "A-4: One model per horizon H in {96, 192, 336, 720}; no multi-output heads (PatchTST convention, proxy).", "A-5: Screening-then-refine strategy with approved executable tier = Phase 1a (60 runs) + Phase 1b (48 runs lower bound) = 108 runs total (locked by researcher approval).", "A-6: Deliverable is a written design only; no live run executed inside this bubblewrap (per task.json and submission contract).", "A-7: RevIN per-instance is acceptable as the working normalization (RevIN-paper proxy, PatchTST-paper proxy).", "A-8: Sanity-band cross-check uses the canonical PatchTST-on-ETTm1 MSE band (MSE ~ 0.29–0.33, MAE ~ 0.34–0.39) at (L=336, H=96) as a proxy threshold; threshold is approximate (PatchTST/TSlib proxy).", "A-9: The dataset split is the official 12mo / 4mo / 4mo for ETTm1 (Informer/paper proxy, TSlib proxy).", "TR-1 (FIXED): Question is 'How do different period settings affect the performance of the PDF model on the ETTm1 dataset?'.", "TR-2 (FIXED): Deliverable is design-only — no claim an experiment was run.", "TR-3 (FIXED): Runtime environment (bubblewrap, no network, CPU, no preinstalled ML stack) is a constraint, not a tunable."], "uncertainties": ["U-1 (RESOLVED, kept as confirmation): 'PDF' identity is locked to PatchTST-family, but remains an assumption because no PDF source code is on disk.", "U-2 (RESOLVED, kept as confirmation): 'period' axis is locked to M-primary / P-secondary / L-covariate, but remains an assumption because no source schema is on disk.", "U-3: No canonical PDF defaults exist on disk; all quoted defaults are PatchTST/TSlib/DLinear/RevIN/Informer proxies — inline markers required to prevent misattribution.", "U-4: A `track.md` could be supplied mid-task from a side channel; design documents the need to re-check against any addition.", "U-5: Out-of-band CPU budget may be tighter than the estimated 250–700 CPU-hour Phase-1 tier; pre-registered priority order (1a → 1b → 1b L-aux → Phase-2 skipped on CPU) mitigates run starvation.", "U-6: ETTm1 data file format is unknown; smoke test recommended at integration time.", "U-7: Metric scope (MSE-only vs. MSE+MAE) is unchecked; current design uses both, the LTSF community standard.", "U-8: Batch convention is locked to '32 windows jointly across channels' but may need to flip to '32 per channel' if the source codebase differs.", "U-9 (new): Phase 1a MSE-magnitude sanity band tolerance default is ±25%; tighten/loosen needs confirmation.", "U-10 (new): Phase 1b tie-breaker defaults to smaller-of-two when M ranks tie; alternate tie-breaker not yet supplied."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-9d8a8a93-0c8a-4281-a84c-b167e1a707f5", "tool_call": {"id": "call-9d8a8a93-0c8a-4281-a84c-b167e1a707f5", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Full design doc for PDF period-sweep on ETTm1\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experiment Design \\u2014 Effect of Period Settings on PDF Model Performance on ETTm1\\n\\n> **Provenance and scope of this document.** `/workspace` contains only `task.json`; no model source code, no default-hyperparameter record, no dataset file, no README/track.md on disk. This design is therefore **design-only** and quotes no values as if read from a PDF repository. Every architectural and hyperparameter default is attributed to its canonical literature source with an inline proxy marker. Any run of this design is out of scope for this task per `task.json` (which states: \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\").\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (a) Research question and pre-registered hypotheses\\n\\n**Research question.** How do different period-related hyperparameter settings affect the forecasting performance of the PDF (PatchTST-family) model on the ETTm1 dataset?\\n\\n**Hypotheses, stated as assumptions about the experiment (not as forecast of outcome):**\\n\\n- **H1 \\u2014 Seasonal-decomposition period `M` matters most on the long horizon.** Best MSE/MAE are obtained when `M` is at or near a divisor/multiple of ETTm1's daily cycle (96 steps at 15-min resolution), and the magnitude of the `M` effect grows with horizon `H`.\\n- **H2 \\u2014 Patch length `P` interacts with `M`.** Best MSE/MAE occur when `P` is a small divisor of `M`, so each patch covers an integer number of seasonal cycles; non-divisory `P` increases aliasing of the decomposition.\\n- **H3 \\u2014 Lookback `L` is a covariate, not a driver.** Once `L \\u2265 2 \\u00d7 max(M)`, varying `L` produces a smaller effect on rank-normalized MSE than varying `M` or `P`.\\n\\nThese hypotheses are **assumptions recorded up front** and will be evaluated by the analysis plan in \\u00a7(f). They are not asserted as true.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (b) Model assumed\\n\\n**Working model.** A PatchTST-style architecture with an **explicit trend/seasonal decomposition block** in the forecasting head \\u2014 i.e., the input time series is patched with length `P` and stride `S`, the patches pass through a Transformer encoder, and the decoder output is split by a moving-average-based series decomposition into seasonal and trend components before being projected to the forecast horizon `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` `[TSlib proxy]`. Per-instance RevIN normalization is applied at the input and reversed at the output `[RevIN-paper proxy]`.\\n\\nConcretely, the components in scope are:\\n\\n| Component | Choice | Source attribution |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Windowing / patching | patches of length `P`, stride `S` along the time axis | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n| Series decomposition | moving-average seasonal-trend split with kernel size `M` applied to the decoder output | `[DLinear-paper proxy]` for the block shape; `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` for the patched encoder feeding into it; `[TSlib proxy]` for the wiring |\\n| Normalization | RevIN per-instance on the encoder input, reversed before loss | `[RevIN-paper proxy]` `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n| Encoder | Transformer, `d_model=128`, `n_heads=16`, `n_layers=3` | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n| Loss | MSE on normalized targets (RevIN-reduced scale) | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` `[TSlib proxy]` |\\n| Output | single-horizon head per `H \\u2208 {96, 192, 336, 720}`, no multi-output heads | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n\\n**Note on re-identification.** If \\\"PDF\\\" turns out to be a different named paper (e.g., a separately published *Periodic Decomposition Forecaster* or *Patch Decomposition Forecaster*), only the following items in this design would need to be revisited, and only minimally: (i) the decomposition block signature (whether it is a moving-average split, a learnable filter, or an FFT-bank reconstruction); (ii) the encoder dimension if the named paper uses a smaller body; (iii) the canonical default for the period knob (single integer vs. integer-valued set); and (iv) the data-loader signature. The sweep grid (M, P, L, seeds, horizons), the fixed controls table, the analysis plan, and the feasibility budget **would be substantively re-used**, because the research question \\u2014 \\\"how does the period setting affect performance\\\" \\u2014 is invariant under the architectural variant.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (c) Data\\n\\n| Field | Value | Source attribution |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Dataset | ETTm1 (15-minute cadence, multivariate) | `[Informer/paper proxy]` for ETTm1 |\\n| Channels | 7 (6 \\\"High UseFul Load\\\" + 1 `OT`) | `[ETTm1 standard]` `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n| Target | `OT` channel for primary MSE/MAE | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n| Train / Val / Test split | 12 months / 4 months / 4 months (official) | `[Informer/paper proxy]` `[TSlib proxy]` |\\n| Loader behaviour | if raw `ETTm1.csv`: load CSV, parse the date column, sort chronologically, treat as a `pandas`-style DataFrame, then slide windows of length `L+H` with stride 1 along time, slicing the last `H` timesteps as the ground-truth forecast and the leading `L` as the input; if pre-windowed tensors `*.npy`/`*.pt`: load with a channel axis preserved (shape `[N, 7, L+H]` or `[N, L+H, 7]`), no further windowing | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` `[TSlib proxy]` |\\n| Boundary padding | **no zero-padding inside the test split**; for sliding the final window across the right edge of the test split, use RevIN with zero-padded left-context (i.e., warp with the last `L` training-side observations mirrored once, never with zeros) to avoid contamination-induced horizon bias | `[RevIN-paper proxy]` `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n| Normalization in loader | RevIN fits the mean/std on each input window only (per-instance) | `[RevIN-paper proxy]` |\\n| Data leakage controls | (i) per-instance RevIN is fit **only** on the input window \\u2014 never on the horizon; (ii) the moving-average decomposition `M` is applied at inference time only to the decoder output, never refit on the test interval; (iii) the training split is contiguous (12 mo), val split contiguous (4 mo, immediately following), test split contiguous (4 mo, immediately following) | `[Informer/paper proxy]` for split policy; `[RevIN-paper proxy]` for per-instance invariance |\\n\\nA concrete **loader pseudocode** (for a future implementer; **not** to be executed in this bubblewrap):\\n\\n```python\\n# Pseudocode, attributed to PatchTST/TSlib/RevIN papers as proxy\\ndef make_windowed_ettm1(csv_or_tensor_path, L, H, stride_train=1, stride_eval=H):\\n    series = load_ettm1(csv_or_tensor_path)            # shape [T, 7] or [7, T]\\n    train, val, test = official_split(series)          # 12mo / 4mo / 4mo\\n    def to_windows(split, stride):\\n        T = len(split); out = []\\n        for s in range(0, T - L - H + 1, stride):\\n            x = split[s : s + L]                       # [L, 7]\\n            y = split[s + L : s + L + H, ot_idx]       # [H]\\n            out.append((x, y))\\n        if T - L - H + 1 <= 0:\\n            # right-edge test window: mirror prior context, no zero-pad\\n            x = np.concatenate([split[max(0, T - 2*L):T - L], split[T - L:]], axis=0)[-L:]\\n            out.append((x, split[max(0, T - H):T, ot_idx]))\\n        return out\\n    return to_windows(train, stride_train), to_windows(val, stride_eval), to_windows(test, stride_eval)\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (d) Fixed controls table\\n\\nThe following table is **held constant for every run**, including Phase 1a, Phase 1b, and Phase 2.\\n\\n| Field | Value | Source attribution / proxy marker |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Dataset | ETTm1 | `[Informer/paper proxy]` |\\n| Channels | 7; target `OT` | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n| Split | 12mo train / 4mo val / 4mo test | `[Informer/paper proxy]` |\\n| Lookback `L` (baseline) | 336 \\u2014 covariate, fixed at this value for Phase 1a/1b unless explicitly varied as covariate | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n| Stride `S` | 8 | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n| Horizons `H` | {96, 192, 336, 720}, one model per `H` | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` `[TSlib proxy]` |\\n| Normalization | RevIN per-instance | `[RevIN-paper proxy]` |\\n| Encoder | Transformer, `d_model=128`, `n_heads=16`, `n_layers=3` | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n| Optimizer | Adam, `lr=1e-3` for the Transformer body | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n| Batch size | 32 (joint across channels; per-batch count, not per-channel) | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` `[TSlib proxy]` |\\n| Epochs | 100 | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n| Early stopping | patience 10 on val MSE | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n| Seeds | {0, 1, 2} | `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` |\\n| Primary metric | MSE on test set | LTSF community standard |\\n| Secondary metric | MAE on test set | LTSF community standard |\\n| Padding policy | no zero-padding inside the test split; mirror prior context at the right edge | `[RevIN-paper proxy]` |\\n| Hardware target (out-of-band) | 24 CPU cores, no GPU; float32 | `[environment constraint]` |\\n\\n> **Proxy attestation.** Every row above that is **not** `environment constraint` or `LTSF community standard` is sourced from PatchTST, DLinear, RevIN, Informer, or Time-Series-Library papers as a documented working baseline. None of these is being asserted as the original PDF repository's choice.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (e) Sweep grid\\n\\nThree phases. Phase 1a is the executable tier inside the screening plan. Phase 1b is the refinement tier. Phase 2 is optional and listed for future use.\\n\\n### Phase 1a \\u2014 Screening at `H = 96`\\n\\n| Knob | Values |\\n|---|---|\\n| Decomposition seasonal period `M` | {24, 48, 96, 144, 288} (covering sub-daily to multi-day at 15-min cadence; 96 \\u2248 1 day) |\\n| Patch length `P` | {8, 16, 24, 32} |\\n| Lookback `L` (covariate, held fixed at this phase) | 336 |\\n| Stride `S` | 8 (fixed) |\\n| Horizon `H` | 96 (fixed) |\\n| Seeds | {0, 1, 2} |\\n\\n**Phase 1a run count.** 5 \\u00d7 4 \\u00d7 3 = **60 single-horizon training runs.**\\n\\n### Phase 1b \\u2014 Top-tier expansion to all four horizons\\n\\nTop-tier definition (pre-registered before unblinding):\\n\\n- Compute rank-averaged MSE across the 60 Phase 1a runs.\\n- Take the **top-2 values of `M`** by mean rank-MSE and the **top-2 values of `P`** by mean rank-MSE.\\n- Take the **top-2 `(M, P)` interactions** by mean rank-MSE.\\n- Combine via a deduplicated cell set: typically 2 M \\u00d7 2 P \\u2192 4 cells, unless the interaction wins force more, capped at 6 cells.\\n\\n| Knob | Values |\\n|---|---|\\n| Cells | up to 6 `(M, P)` top-tier cells from Phase 1a |\\n| Lookback `L` | 336 (fixed covariate baseline; **Optionally expand L with one secondary value**, see below) |\\n| Horizon `H` | {96, 192, 336, 720} |\\n| Seeds | {0, 1, 2} |\\n\\n**Phase 1b run count.**\\n\\n- If 4 cells: 4 \\u00d7 4 \\u00d7 3 = 48 runs.\\n- If 5 cells: 5 \\u00d7 4 \\u00d7 3 = 60 runs.\\n- If 6 cells: 6 \\u00d7 4 \\u00d7 3 = 72 runs.\\n\\nThe actual run count for Phase 1b is between **48 and 72**, depending on top-tier tie-breakers. **Planning budget = 48 runs (lower bound = 4 cells), giving an executable tier of 60 + 48 = 108 runs**, which is the figure approved in the plan.\\n\\n> **L-auxiliary measurement (optional, inside Phase 1b).** At the single top-tier `(M, P)` cell, additionally train at `L \\u2208 {96, 192, 336, 720}` to test H3 directly. Adds at most 1 \\u00d7 4 L \\u00d7 3 seeds = **12 auxiliary runs**. Reported as a covariate, not a primary sweep axis.\\n\\n### Phase 2 \\u2014 Optional FFT-top-k and stride refinement (NOT in the executable tier; for future use)\\n\\n| Knob | Values (only at Phase-1b top-tier cells) |\\n|---|---|\\n| FFT top-k | {1, 3, 5} (TimesNet-style, for benchmarking only) |\\n| Stride `S` | {4, 8, 16} (folded in here, not in Phase 1a/1b) |\\n| Horizons `H` | all four |\\n| Seeds | {0, 1, 2} |\\n\\n**Phase 2 run count.** Up to 6 cells \\u00d7 3 top-k \\u00d7 3 stride \\u00d7 4 H \\u00d7 3 seeds = **648 runs**, listed here only to bound the size of any future expansion; this is **not within the approved executable tier of 108 runs** and would require compute outside this task.\\n\\n### Total approved budget\\n\\n- **Approved executable tier:** 60 (Phase 1a) + 48 (Phase 1b lower bound) = **108 single-horizon runs**.\\n- This is the figure the design should be evaluated against per the submission contract (design-only).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (f) Analysis plan\\n\\nThe analysis is gated on Phase 1a / Phase 1b / (optional) Phase 2 results being available out-of-band; this design only specifies *what would be computed*, not that it was computed.\\n\\n1. **Main-effect plot (MSE and MAE vs. `M`).**\\n   - x-axis: `M` \\u2208 {24, 48, 96, 144, 288}.\\n   - y-axis: test-set MSE (or MAE).\\n   - Line color: `P` (4 levels).\\n   - Facets: one panel per `(H, L)` combination.\\n   - y-error bars: standard deviation across the 3 seeds.\\n\\n2. **Interaction heat-maps of `(M, P)`.**\\n   - For each `(H, L)` combination, draw a 5 \\u00d7 4 heat-map with `M` on the y-axis, `P` on the x-axis, cell value = mean MSE across seeds.\\n   - Secondary heat-map with rank-normalized MSE (see step 4).\\n\\n3. **Per-horizon breakdown.**\\n   - The same heat-maps and main-effect plots faceted by `H \\u2208 {96, 192, 336, 720}` to surface any `M \\u00d7 H` interaction. Long-horizon runs are weighted equally only after rank normalization.\\n\\n4. **Rank normalization within horizon.**\\n   - For each horizon `H`, rank all 60 (Phase 1a) cells by mean MSE across seeds; convert ranks to `[0, 1]` (or to percentiles). Use rank-normalized values for any cross-horizon aggregation or \\\"main effect averaging\\\" so the long-horizon runs do not dominate.\\n   - When reporting any cross-horizon summary statistic, use rank-normalized values.\\n\\n5. **Statistical tests.**\\n   - **Primary test: paired Wilcoxon signed-rank.** For each fixed `(P, L, H)` configuration, pair the seeds across the levels of `M` and apply a Wilcoxon test on seed-mean MSE. Report an effect-size (rank-biserial correlation) alongside the p-value.\\n   - **Multi-comparison:** Benjamini\\u2013Hochberg correction across the in-hypothesis test family (per `(P, H)` strip), reporting `q`-values. Pre-register the alpha = 0.05 threshold for the BH rejection.\\n   - **Secondary test:** a 4-way repeated-measures ANOVA over `(M, P, H, seed)` to detect interaction effects; **report as exploratory** because the residual degrees of freedom with 3 seeds are limited.\\n   - **Pre-registered nulls:**\\n     - H1: alternative = \\\"MSE differs in `M`\\\", aggregate test.\\n     - H2: alternative = \\\"non-additive interaction of `M` \\u00d7 `P`\\\", interaction term in the ANOVA.\\n     - H3: alternative = \\\"MSE changes with `L` at top `(M, P)`\\\", Wilcoxon on the L-auxiliary batch (Phase 1b optional extension).\\n\\n6. **Sanity-band cross-check.**\\n   - Confirm that the **MSE magnitudes lie within the canonical PatchTST-on-ETTm1 band** for the chosen architecture at the working-baseline `(P=16, M=24, L=336, H=96, lr=1e-3, RevIN)` configuration. The expected band, sourced from the PatchTST and Time-Series-Library reports: `MSE \\u2248 0.29\\u20130.33` and `MAE \\u2248 0.34\\u20130.39` on ETTm1 at `(L=336, H=96)` `[PatchTST-paper proxy]` `[TSlib proxy]`. If the in-experiment value falls outside this band by more than ~25%, flag the configuration as suspect (likely a code-bug rather than a real PDF effect) before reporting the main effect.\\n   - The sanity threshold is a proxy and double-coded; it is *not* an experimental claim.\\n\\n7. **Reporting artifacts (planned, not produced here).**\\n   - One CSV with columns `{H, L, M, P, seed, MSE, MAE, rank_MSE_H}` for the entire run matrix.\\n   - PNG main-effect plot and `(M, P)` heat-map for each `(H, L)` panel.\\n   - JSON summary file with the BH-corrected Wilcoxon q-values per hypothesis per horizon.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (g) Feasibility in the 24-CPU bubblewrap\\n\\n- The bubblewrap has **no scientific/ML stack installed** (only `PyYAML 6.0.1` is present in the relevant subset of `pip list`; no numpy, pandas, scipy, scikit-learn, statsmodels, matplotlib, torch, or JAX). Network is disabled, so no `pip install` can be performed inside the bubble.\\n- Therefore, this design document is itself the deliverable for this turn (per `task.json` \\\"design-only\\\" and the submission contract). **No live run is claimed and none can be performed inside this environment.**\\n- **Out-of-band feasibility** for the executable tier of 108 runs:\\n  - On a CPU-only box (24 threads, no GPU), a single-horizon PatchTST on ETTm1 with `L=336`, `H \\u2264 720` is on the order of tens of minutes per epoch at the smaller horizon and grows to hours at `H=720`. With the early-stopping budget of 100 epochs / patience 10, realistic per-run cost is 1\\u20136 CPU-hours at `H=96` and 3\\u201312 CPU-hours at `H=720`.\\n  - The Phase 1a + Phase 1b 108-run tier is therefore estimated at ~250\\u2013700 CPU-hours of training (assuming most runs early-stop near patience 10). This is feasible in a few days on a 24-CPU box with PyTorch.\\n  - The Phase-1b L-auxiliary 12-run extension is an extra ~50\\u2013150 CPU-hours.\\n  - The optional Phase 2 (~648 runs) is **not recommended** on CPU alone; it would need a GPU cluster.\\n- The design document does not depend on a particular ML stack \\u2014 only the loader pseudocode, fixed-controls table, sweep grid, and analysis plan. These can be drafted without any framework installed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (h) Assumptions and risks\\n\\nCarried forward from the U-list and labelled inline:\\n\\n- **U-1 (resolved, kept as confirmation): 'PDF' = PatchTST-family with explicit decomposition block, locked by researcher.** Assumption; if a named paper is supplied later, only the architecture subscript in \\u00a7(b) and the decomposition-block signature need revision.\\n- **U-2 (resolved, kept as confirmation): 'period' axis = `M` (primary), `P` (secondary), `L` (covariate), locked by researcher.** Assumption; if the codebase later exposes `period` as a different axis, the sweep must re-rank.\\n- **U-3: No canonical PDF defaults exist on disk.** All baselines quoted in this design are PatchTST, DLinear, RevIN, Informer, or Time-Series-Library paper proxies, marked inline. **Risk:** misattributing proxy defaults to PDF \\u2014 mitigated by the inline `[proxy]` markers, which are required at every claim.\\n- **U-4: No `track.md` exists on disk** (only a string inside `task.json`). **Risk:** the researcher could supply additional cue material mid-task; the design should be re-checked against any such addition.\\n- **U-5: Out-of-band CPU budget may be smaller than the 250\\u2013700 CPU-hour Phase-1 estimate.** **Risk:** run starvation \\u2192 incomplete main-effect plots. **Mitigation:** pre-register a strict priority order: Phase 1a first; Phase 1b second (only at cells whose Phase 1a MSE lies inside the top-quartile of rank-MSE); Phase 1b L-auxiliary third; Phase 2 not at all on CPU.\\n- **U-6: ETTm1 data file format unknown (`ETTm1.csv` vs. pre-windowed tensors).** **Risk:** loader bug at the boundary-pad step. **Mitigation:** the loader pseudocode in \\u00a7(c) handles both; a 1-row smoke test (load, slice a single window, verify shape `[L, 7]` and `[H]` for OT) is the recommended first integration step.\\n- **U-7: Metric scope unclear.** **Risk:** if the source expects MSE only, the MAE column is dead weight; if it expects both, dropping MAE is a defect. **Mitigation:** keep both MSE (primary) and MAE (secondary) by default; this is the LTSF community standard.\\n- **U-8: Batch convention unclear.** **Risk:** \\\"batch 32\\\" can mean 32 windows (joint across channels, default here) or 32 windows per channel. **Mitigation:** the design locks the joint convention; the open questions section asks the researcher to confirm.\\n\\n**Cross-cutting risks** (additional to U-1..U-8):\\n\\n- **Confounding between `P` and token count.** Different `(P, S)` choices change the number of tokens, which changes the encoder's effective depth and the optimizer's dynamics. **Mitigation:** report token counts in the CSV and as a covariate in \\u00a7(f) step 5 if the ANOVA finds a `M \\u00d7 P` interaction.\\n- **Horizon asymmetry dominates MSE magnitude.** Without rank normalization the main-effect plot mirrors `H=720`. **Mitigation:** step 4 in \\u00a7(f) is non-optional.\\n- **Three seeds give limited paired-test power.** Wilcoxon at 3-seed pairing is exact only for some configurations; for small effects, two-seed pilot followed by a power calculation may be needed before a third seed. Pre-registered option: collapse to median over seeds and report IQR if a third seed run is not feasible.\\n- **No live integration test inside this bubblewrap.** **Risk:** silent loader/decoder bugs. **Mitigation (out-of-band):** formal smoke test of the loader + one 1-epoch training step at `(L=336, P=16, M=24, H=96, lr=1e-3)` before launching the Phase 1a grid.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Open questions for the researcher (if a run is later attempted)\\n\\nThese items would still need one-line confirmation before the executable tier is launched in a future out-of-band environment; they are explicitly **not blocking** the design.\\n\\n1. **ETTm1 file format.** Original `ETTm1.csv` with date column, or pre-windowed tensors (`*.npy` / `*.pt`)? \\u2014 needed for the loader smoke test.\\n2. **LR for a DLinear-style body.** If the codebase variant of PDF swaps the Transformer encoder for a DLinear-style linear body, the optimizer LR should drop from `1e-3` to `1e-4` `[DLinear-paper proxy]`. Confirm which body the PDF implementation actually uses.\\n3. **Batch convention.** \\\"Batch 32\\\" in \\u00a7(d) is locked as 32 windows jointly across channels. Confirm if the codebase instead uses \\\"32 per channel\\\".\\n4. **Phase 1b tie-breaker.** When two `M` values produce equal rank-MSE inside Phase 1a, the design keeps the smaller of the two. Confirm or supply an alternate tie-breaker.\\n5. **Whether an additional `track.md`** with original PDF defaults is expected from the researcher mid-task; none exists on disk now.\\n6. **Whether MSE only, MAE only, or both.** The design locks MSE (primary) + MAE (secondary); confirm.\\n7. **Tolerance for the canonical-PatchTST sanity band** in \\u00a7(f) step 6 (default \\u00b125%). Tighten or loosen if a stricter experimental prior is required.\\n\\n---\\n\\n> **Closing attestation.** This document is a design, not a run. Every value attributed to a paper is a documented working baseline marked as such. No experiment was performed, no result was fabricated.\", \"evidence\": \"Evidence base carried in from earlier turns (no new commands needed to finalize a design-only document; no source files appeared, no dependencies installed):\\n\\n- `ls -la /workspace` and `find /workspace -maxdepth 10` show only `/workspace/task.json` \\u2014 no source, no data, no README, no `track.md`.\\n- `inspect_repository /workspace/task.json` quoted the only authorable source document (`question`, `context`, `documents.track.md` = \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\").\\n- `python3 -m pip list | grep -i -E \\\